#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059382 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059823 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb626c84-db31-5e1b-90cb-3f5d13ee0af5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals From Large Samples"}] :question/id #uuid "97c8f1dc-747e-5aa4-ab4f-eb809abb560e" :question/math-academy-id "e-10035" :question/problem "Consider a sample of size $n=196$ from a population with standard deviation $\\sigma=7$. Given that the mean of the sample is $\\overline{x}=24$, find a $95\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ of the distribution.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z > 1.96) = 0.025$, where $Z \\sim N(0,1)$, as shown in the diagram below.*\n\n![](images/28/28f56656e191e342a4815ba74bdd29cba5247fba7d8ab0feca8ffbe0b785ab8c.png)" :question/worked-solution "We do not know the distribution of the population. However, the sample size\n\n$$\nn = 196 \\geq 30\n$$\n\nis *sufficiently large*. Therefore, according to the central limit theorem, we may use the following approximation:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\overline{X} - \\mu}{\\sigma/\\sqrt n} \\sim N(0,1)\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\overline{X}$ is the sample mean,\n- $\\mu$ is the population mean,\n- $\\sigma^2$ is the population variance, and\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1$, the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overline{x} \\pm z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nP(Z > z_{\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}\n$$\n\nand $Z \\sim N(0,1)$.\n\nWe are interested in finding a $95\\%$ confidence interval. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha=1-0.95=0.05 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}=0.025\n$$\n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\nP(Z > 1.96) = 0.025\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\nz_{\\alpha/2} = z_{0.025} = 1.96\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $95\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ is the following:\n\n$$\n24 ± 1.96 \\cdot \\frac{7}{\\sqrt{196}}; 24 ± 0.98\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059383 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059824 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "27d20d28-3fba-5830-8bff-76e9a1c24bec" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals: Applications"}] :question/id #uuid "3189a42a-200e-5caa-bc76-6ac1f4ae424d" :question/math-academy-id "e-10039" :question/problem "The daily percentage return of a particular stock has a population standard deviation of $\\sigma = 5\\%$. A sample of $90$ randomly selected trading days was examined, and it was found that the mean daily return of the stock over these days was $7.5\\%$. Find a $90\\%$ confidence interval for the mean daily percentage return $\\mu$ of the entire population of trading days.\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z \\sim N(0,1)$, the table below shows the $z$-scores $z_p$ such that $P(Z > z_p) = p$ for some particular values of $p$.*\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $z_p$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |" :question/worked-solution "We do not know the distribution of the population. However, the sample size\n\n$$\nn = 90\\geq 30\n$$\n\nis *sufficiently large*. Therefore, according to the central limit theorem, we may use the following approximation:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\overline{X} - \\mu}{\\sigma/\\sqrt n} \\sim N(0,1)\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\overline{X}$ is the sample mean,\n- $\\mu$ is the population mean,\n- $\\sigma^2$ is the population variance, and\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1$, the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overline{x} \\pm z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nP(Z > z_{\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}\n$$\n\nand $Z \\sim N(0,1)$.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overline{x}=7.5, \\qquad n = 90, \\qquad \\sigma = 5\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $90\\%$ confidence interval. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha=1-0.9=0.1 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}=0.05\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $z$-score value $z_{0.05}$ such that\n\n$$\nP(Z > z_{0.05}) = 0.05\n$$\n\nwhere $Z \\sim N(0,1)$.\n\n![](images/2f/2fc6327df99c6b88508b0c4de2e4edf6c5521974f05e2e2f02d09786937285a7.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the normal distribution, we obtain that\n\n$$\nz_{0.05}=1.645{:}\n$$\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0.050}$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $z_p$ | $1.282$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}1.645}$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\nTherefore, the $90\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ is the following:\n\n$$\n7.5 ± 1.645 \\cdot \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{90}}; 7.5 ± 0.867\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059384 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059825 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b90997d0-b563-5541-9fe2-36d7457da7b5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals From Normal Populations"}] :question/id #uuid "3a166c80-cc70-5dd3-9dbe-2798dc882199" :question/math-academy-id "e-10046" :question/problem "Consider a sample of size $n=9$ from a normal population with standard deviation $\\sigma=2$. Given that the mean of the sample is $\\overline{x}=-5$, find a $90\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ of the distribution.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z > 1.645) = 0.05$, where $Z \\sim N(0,1)$, as shown in the diagram below.*\n\n![](images/39/39a83b1081d22919c9549c4ff1c6ad8006dc802cc23f9802d762e97883bea48c.png)" :question/worked-solution "We are told that the population is normally distributed. Therefore, the distribution of sample mean is also normal, and we have that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\overline{X} - \\mu}{\\sigma/\\sqrt n} \\sim N(0,1)\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\overline{X}$ is the sample mean,\n- $\\mu$ is the population mean,\n- $\\sigma^2$ is the population variance, and\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1$, the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overline{x} \\pm z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nP(Z > z_{\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}\n$$\n\nand $Z \\sim N(0,1)$.\n\nWe are interested in finding a $90\\%$ confidence interval. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha=1-0.9=0.1 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}=0.05\n$$\n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\nP(Z > 1.645) = 0.05\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\nz_{\\alpha/2} = z_{0.05} = 1.645\n$$\n\nTherefore, a $90\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ is the following:\n\n$$\n-5 ± 1.645 \\cdot \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{9}}; - 5 ± 1.097\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060050 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059823 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb626c84-db31-5e1b-90cb-3f5d13ee0af5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals From Large Samples"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aea98a54-d931-5e46-9213-ed088bf2330e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25 ± 1.96" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056659} {:answer/id #uuid "02e285dd-73a0-523d-94b9-c603f3104387" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14 ± 2.56" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056660} {:answer/id #uuid "13acbb03-a7fc-5d0d-a9fd-7ed30b64c8d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25 ± 0.98" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056661} {:answer/id #uuid "3a7fd1db-029c-5a7d-ab90-4a74fccd6a3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14 ± 1.96" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056662} {:answer/id #uuid "71412ec0-3e3f-5a07-9661-bc231f112fed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident 